  "articleBody": "When altering an interior layout, simple framing oversights can trigger failed city inspections and cause costly delays. Keeping your remodel compliant means catching structural and code requirements early.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid:\n- Removing walls without temporary shoring: Knocking out studs before installing temporary support compromises floor and ceiling loads above. Always secure the structure first.\n- Enclosing framing before rough trade inspections: Putting up drywall before inspectors verify framing, fire blocking, and rough mechanical work leads to forced demolition.\n- Neglecting mechanical coordination: Cutting structural joists or studs improperly for pipes or wiring weakens members. Coordinate ductwork and conduits alongside the framing plan.\n- Overlooking fire blocking and draft stopping: Code requires specific fire-rated separations inside stud cavities. Skipping these details stops inspections cold.\n- Guessing on load paths: Modifying truss systems or structural spans without verified calculations creates long-term sagging.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps:\nWorking with a contractor who coordinates framing alongside sub-trades prevents rework and keeps inspectors satisfied at every phase.\n\nPlanning an interior remodel in the Denver area? Call Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 for experienced, hands-on framing support.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
